Setting a property: two XAML syntaxes
Attribute syntax (the one we’ll use almost everywhere):
&lt;Label Text="Hello!" /&gt;
Property-element syntax (rarer, but you’ll see it soon):
&lt;Label&gt;
    &lt;Label.Text&gt;Hello!&lt;/Label.Text&gt;
&lt;/Label&gt;
Both set the exact same Text property, to the exact same value.
Both Label tags are closed “/” is there on both.
Why would you ever use the longer one?
Attribute syntax only works when the value can be written as a simple string.
Property-element syntax is needed when the value is something more complex than a string — a list, or another object.

MauiProgram.cs
MauiProgram.cs is where the MAUI application is configured. (For now, leave it alone!)
For example:
public static class MauiProgram
{
    public static MauiApp CreateMauiApp() {
        var builder = MauiApp.CreateBuilder();
        builder
            .UseMauiApp&lt;App&gt;()
            .ConfigureFonts(fonts =&gt;
            {
                fonts.AddFont("OpenSans-Regular.ttf", "OpenSansRegular");
                fonts.AddFont("OpenSans-Semibold.ttf", "OpenSansSemibold");
            });

#if DEBUG
        builder.Logging.AddDebug();
#endif

        return builder.Build();
    }
}
For now
Think of this as:

Where we configure the MAUI application.

MainPage.xaml
This is where we describe the UI for our page.
For example:
&lt;ContentPage
    xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/dotnet/2021/maui"
    x:Class="MyMauiApp.MainPage"&gt;

    &lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
        &lt;Label Text="Hello!" /&gt;
    &lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;

&lt;/ContentPage&gt;

MainPage.xaml.cs
The .xaml.cs file is the code-behind.
This is where we can write C# code associated with the page.
namespace MyMauiApp;

public partial class MainPage : ContentPage
{
    public MainPage()
    {
        InitializeComponent();
    }
}
XAML + code-behind
These two files work together to make the MainPage class. This is where we are going to be doing all our editing for the first few weeks at least.
You may have to click the arrow next to the MainPage.xaml in Visual Studio to actually see and access the code-behind file.

What does partial mean?
Normally, a class is written in one place:
public class Person
{
    // ...
}
With partial, one class can be split across multiple files.
public partial class MainPage : ContentPage
{
    // C# code
}
The XAML describes another part of that same class.
So:
MainPage.xaml



MainPage.xaml.cs
=
MainPage

InitializeComponent()
You will normally see this in the page constructor:
public MainPage()
{
    InitializeComponent();
}
This initialises the UI described in the XAML.
In simple terms

“Set up the objects described by my XAML.”







Warning


Never delete InitializeComponent() from your code, even if the compiler says there is a problem.
This is required to Initialise all the XAML components, so if you delete this none of the XAML controls will show.
If InitializeComponent() has a compiler error, this is almost always an issue in the XAML code.

Layouts
Every page must have one Parent layout.
You cannot have
&lt;ContentPage&gt;
&lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
&lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;
&lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
&lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;

&lt;/ContentPage&gt;
as those two Vertical Stack layouts are on the same level.
Same with
&lt;ContentPage&gt;
&lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
&lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;
&lt;Grid&gt;
&lt;/Grid&gt;
&lt;/ContentPage&gt;
But layouts can be children
&lt;ContentPage&gt;
&lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
    &lt;Grid&gt;
    &lt;/Grid&gt;
&lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;

&lt;/ContentPage&gt;

VerticalStackLayout
Places its children vertically.
&lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;

    &lt;Label Text="First" /&gt;
    &lt;Label Text="Second" /&gt;
    &lt;Button Text="Third" /&gt;

&lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;
Result:
First

Second

[ Third ]
Useful properties

Spacing
Padding
HorizontalOptions
VerticalOptions

HorizontalStackLayout
Places its children horizontally.
&lt;HorizontalStackLayout&gt;

    &lt;Button Text="Yes" /&gt;
    &lt;Button Text="No" /&gt;
    &lt;Button Text="Cancel" /&gt;

&lt;/HorizontalStackLayout&gt;
Result:
[ Yes ]   [ No ]   [ Cancel ]
Useful properties

Spacing
Padding
HorizontalOptions
VerticalOptions

ScrollView
What happens when the content is larger than the available screen?
A ScrollView allows the user to scroll through content that does not fit.
&lt;ScrollView&gt;
    &lt;VerticalStackLayout&gt;
        &lt;Label
            Text="First item" /&gt;
        &lt;Label
            Text="Second item" /&gt;
        &lt;Label
            Text="Third item" /&gt;
        &lt;!-- lots more content --&gt;
    &lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;
&lt;/ScrollView&gt;

ScrollView = a window onto content that may be larger than the available space.

The content inside the ScrollView can extend beyond the visible area.
The user can then scroll to see it.
Important
A ScrollView should normally contain one child — usually a layout such as a VerticalStackLayout or Grid.
It is particularly useful for forms and pages with lots of content.

Displaying collections
So far we have created individual controls:
&lt;Label Text="One" /&gt;
&lt;Label Text="Two" /&gt;
&lt;Label Text="Three" /&gt;
But what if we have hundreds of items?
We need a control designed to display a collection.
Enter CollectionView

CollectionView
CollectionView displays a collection of items.
It can display items:

vertically
horizontally
in a grid

A simple example:
&lt;CollectionView&gt;
    &lt;CollectionView.ItemsSource&gt;
        &lt;x:Array Type="{x:Type x:String}"&gt;
            &lt;x:String&gt;Apple&lt;/x:String&gt;
            &lt;x:String&gt;Banana&lt;/x:String&gt;
            &lt;x:String&gt;Orange&lt;/x:String&gt;
        &lt;/x:Array&gt;
    &lt;/CollectionView.ItemsSource&gt;

    &lt;CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
        &lt;DataTemplate&gt;
            &lt;Label
                Text="{Binding .}"
                Padding="10" /&gt;
        &lt;/DataTemplate&gt;
    &lt;/CollectionView.ItemTemplate&gt;
&lt;/CollectionView&gt;

Creating a Label in C#
We don’t have to use XAML.
The same type of object can be created directly in C#:
Label label = new Label
{
    Text = "Hello!"
};
We can then add it to a layout:
&lt;VerticalStackLayout
    x:Name="MainVerticalLayout"&gt;
    &lt;!--

    --&gt;
&lt;/VerticalStackLayout&gt;
MainVerticalLayout.Add(label);






Warning


new Label()
creates a new Label object.
Every time that statement runs, a new object is created.
So if you’re writing loops, be careful!

Dynamic UI
Because controls are objects, we can create them dynamically.
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 5; i++)
{
    Label label = new Label
    {
        Text = $"Item {i}"
    };

    MainVerticalLayout.Add(label);
}
The application creates five separate Label objects.
This is useful when the UI depends on runtime information.

Which Language?
Consider this familiar example:
char gradeEarned;

if (num &gt;= 70)
{
    gradeEarned = 'A';
}
else if (num &gt;= 60)
{
    gradeEarned = 'B';
}
else
{
    gradeEarned = 'C';
}

C# vs Java
Arrays are also very similar.
For example, suppose we have:
int[] listOfInt = { 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 };
The traditional for loop is also very similar.
Java:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; listOfInt.length; ++i)
{
    System.out.println(listOfInt[i]);
}
C#:
for (int i = 0; i &lt; listOfInt.Length; ++i)
{
    Console.WriteLine(listOfInt[i]);
}
Notice two small differences:

Java uses .length
C# uses .Length

Length is a property in C#.

The Ternary Conditional Operator
Sometimes we have a very simple if/else.
For example:
string result;

if (num &gt;= 50)
{
    result = "Pass";
}
else
{
    result = "Fail";
}
Most languages allows us to write this using the ternary conditional operator:
string result = num &gt;= 50 ? "Pass" : "Fail";
The general form is:
condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false
This is useful when the decision is simple.
Don’t use it to turn complicated logic into an unreadable mess.

String Formatting
We also have several ways of putting values into strings.
One familiar approach is concatenation:
string name = "Donny";
int age = 25;

Console.WriteLine("Name: " + name + ", Age: " + age);
But C# also provides string interpolation.
Console.WriteLine($"Name: {name}, Age: {age}");
The $ tells C# that the string contains interpolated expressions.

String Interpolation
Expressions can be placed directly inside { }.
string name = "Donny";
int age = 25;

Console.WriteLine($"Hello {name}");
Console.WriteLine($"You are {age} years old");
You can also put expressions inside.
Console.WriteLine($"Next year you will be {age + 1}");
This is likely to be much more convenient than constructing long strings using +.

Naming Conventions in C#
PascalCase
Use PascalCase for:

Classes
Methods
Properties
Public members

Each word starts with a capital letter.
public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public void IncreaseAge()
    {
    }
}
Examples:
Person
StudentAccount
CalculateAverage
FirstName

Access Modifiers: internal Classes
You already know public and private from Java.
C# adds a third one worth knowing: internal.
When you create a new project, some of the generated template code uses it on classes:
internal class Program
{
    // ...
}
An internal class is only visible within the same project.
Code in a different project can’t see or use it at all — not even to reference the class name.
public class Person   // visible to other projects too
{
}

internal class Helper // only visible inside this project
{
}

Namespaces
A namespace groups related classes together and helps avoid naming collisions.
namespace SchoolSystem
{
    public class Person
    {
    }
}
Two different libraries could each have their own Person class, as long as they live in different namespaces:
SchoolSystem.Person
HospitalSystem.Person
Using a namespace
Rather than writing the full name every time, we bring a namespace into scope with using:
using SchoolSystem;

Person person = new Person();
Can also do
namespace SchoolSystem;

public class Person{
}
Then the namespace applies to the whole file

Classes and Encapsulation
A class allows us to group related:

Data
Methods
Functionality

For example:
public class Person
{
    private string _name;
    private int _age;

    public void SayHello()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Hello!");
    }
}
The private fields are hidden from code outside the class.
This is encapsulation.
In a larger application, this allows a class to control how its data is accessed and changed.

Properties
In Java, you may have written:
private String name;

public void setName(String name)
{
    this.name = name;
}

public String getName()
{
    return name;
}
The methods control access to the private field.
This will work fine in C#, but it is not the usual way of doing things.
C# has a different mechanism for this: properties.

C# Properties
A property allows us to expose controlled access to data. A property will have a backing field that stores the data.
public class Person
{
    private string _name;

    public string Name
    {
        get
        {
            return _name;
        }

        set
        {
            _name = value;
        }
    }
}
A property has accessors.
The get accessor is used when reading the property.
The set accessor is used when assigning to it.
The private field _name is where the actual data is stored.

Using a Property
Instead of:
person.setName("Donny");
System.out.print(person.getName());
we can write:
person.Name = "Donny";

Console.Write(person.Name);
C# knows whether we are reading or writing the property.
person.Name = "Donny";  // set

Console.Write(person.Name);  // get
The property looks like a variable when we use it.

Auto-Implemented Properties
If we don’t need to write our own get and set logic, C# provides a shorthand.
public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }
}
This is an auto-implemented property.
C# automatically provides the backing storage for us. The private field does still exist, we just don’t have a reference to it.
This is extremely common in C#.

Read-Only Properties
We don’t always want the outside world to be able to change a value.
For example:
public class Person
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public int Age { get; }
}
A property with only a get accessor cannot normally be assigned from outside the class.
The value can be assigned when the object is constructed:
public Person(int age)
{
    Age = age;
}
But it cannot subsequently be changed:
Person person = new Person(25);

person.Age = 26;
Think of this as:

“You can ask me what my age is, but you cannot change it.”

Private Setters
Sometimes we want the class itself to be able to change a value, but we don’t want other code to do so.
public class Person
{
    public int Age { get; private set; }

    public Person(int age)
    {
        Age = age;
    }

    public void IncreaseAge()
    {
        ++Age;
    }
}
Outside the class:
Person person = new Person(25);

Console.WriteLine(person.Age);  // ✅

person.Age = 30;                // ❌
But the class itself can change the property:
person.IncreaseAge();           // ✅

Properties vs Fields
It is important to distinguish between a field and a property.
private int _age;               // field

public int Age { get; set; }    // property
A field is a variable belonging to the object.
A property provides controlled access to data.
The property may use a field behind the scenes:
private int _age;

public int Age
{
    get
    {
        return _age;
    }

    set
    {
        _age = value;
    }
}
Or C# can generate the backing storage automatically:
public int Age { get; set; }

Why Properties Matter
Properties give us encapsulation.
We can start with:
public string Name { get; set; }
But later we might need validation:
private int _age;

public int Age
{
    get
    {
        return _age;
    }

    set
    {
        if (value &gt;= 0)
        {
            _age = value;
        }
    }
}
Here the property controls access to _age.
The setter can validate the value before changing the field.

Inheritance
C# uses : to specify inheritance.
Java:
public class Dog extends Animal
{
}
C#:
public class Dog : Animal
{
}
A derived class inherits members from its base class.

The base Class
Sometimes a derived class needs to access functionality from its base class.
C# uses the keyword:
base
(In Java you would’ve used super) For example:
public class Animal
{
    public string Name { get; set; }

    public void Speak()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some animal noise");
    }
}
A derived class can call the base implementation:
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public void SpeakLikeADog()
    {
        base.Speak();
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
}
base means: “the version belonging to my base class.”

Constructors and base
base can also be used when calling a base-class constructor.
public class Animal
{
    public Animal(string name)
    {
        Name = name;
    }

    public string Name { get; }
}
The derived class can call it:
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public Dog(string name)  : base(name)
    {

    }
}
The Dog constructor passes name to the Animal constructor.

Overriding Methods
Sometimes a derived class needs to provide its own version of a method.
In Java, you may remember:
@Override
public void Speak()
{
    System.out.println("Woof!");
}
In C#, you cannot override just any method.
The base class must give permission by marking the method as virtual.
public class Animal
{
    public virtual void Speak()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Some animal noise");
    }
}
The derived class can then use override:
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public override void Speak()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
    }
}
Think of it as:

Java asks for forgiveness.
C# asks for permission.

virtual and override
The base class:
public virtual void Speak()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Some animal noise");
}
is saying:

“Derived classes are allowed to provide their own implementation of this method.”

The derived class:
public override void Speak()
{
    Console.WriteLine("Woof!");
}
is saying:

“I am providing that alternative implementation.”

base and override
An override doesn’t necessarily mean we have to completely throw away the base implementation.
We can call it using base.
public class Dog : Animal
{
    public override void Speak()
    {
        base.Speak();
        Console.WriteLine("...and then Woof!");
    }
}
So:
base.Speak();
means:
Call the implementation from my base class.

Putting It Together
A simple C# class might therefore look like:
public class Person
{
    private string _name;

    public string Name
    {
        get
        {
            return _name;
        }

        private set
        {
            _name = value;
        }
    }

    public int Age { get; private set; }

    public Person(string name, int age)
    {
        _name = name;
        Age = age;
    }

    public void IncreaseAge()
    {
        ++Age;
    }

    public void SayHello()
    {
        Console.WriteLine($"Hello, my name is {Name} and I am {Age}.");
    }
}
